<header><p class="st-kicker">emirate · Egypt, North Africa and Sudan</p><h1 class="st-title">Tulunid emirate</h1><p class="st-meta">868 CE – 905 CE · capital al-Qata'i</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The first autonomous dynasty of Islamic Egypt, founded by Ahmad ibn Tulun, builder of Cairo's great mosque.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ahmad-ibn-tulun">Ahmad ibn Tulun</a>, son of a Turkish slave soldier at the Abbasid court in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samarra">Samarra</a>, was sent to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> as deputy governor in 868. He took control of the province's finances, built his own army of Turkish and African slave soldiers and, while still recognising the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/abbasid-caliphate">Abbasid Caliphate</a>, made Egypt effectively independent. In 878 he occupied <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/syrian-arab-republic">Syria</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ahmad-ibn-tulun">Ibn Tulun</a> founded a new capital, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-qatai">al-Qata'i</a>, north of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fustat">Fustat</a>, with a palace, hospital and the great <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mosque-of-ibn-tulun">Mosque of Ibn Tulun</a>, completed in 879 and still standing. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt's</a> revenue now stayed largely in the country, funding irrigation and building. His son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khumarawayh">Khumarawayh</a> made a treaty with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caliphate">caliph</a> in 886 and married his daughter <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qatr-al-nada">Qatr al-Nada</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-mutadid">al-Mu'tadid</a> with a famously lavish dowry.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>Extravagance and the murder of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khumarawayh">Khumarawayh</a> in 896 left the dynasty weak. In 905 an Abbasid army under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/muhammad-ibn-sulayman-al-katib">Muhammad ibn Sulayman</a> retook <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> and razed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-qatai">al-Qata'i</a>, except for the mosque.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/tulunid-emirate.jpg" alt="Tulunid emirate" width="320" height="229"><figcaption>Image: Ro4444 · CC BY 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Tulunids_893.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>868 CE – 905 CE</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/9th-century">9th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/10th-century">10th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>emirate</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>bureaucratic</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>al-Qata'i</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Ahmad ibn Tulun, Khumarawayh</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Sunni Islam</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Arabic</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/egypt">Egypt</a>, <a href="/countries/syria">Syria</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Tulunids, Tulunid dynasty</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=886&amp;focus=tulunid-emirate" aria-label="Tulunid emirate on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5491 1082 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Tulunid emirate lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5811 1338L6134 1224"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M6134 1224h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5811 1338h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5811 1338h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks its capital. <a href="/atlas?year=886&amp;focus=tulunid-emirate">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>

